The defect, verbatim from the issues. #44 AC1: "`run_function_profiling` has no `two_sign` argument.
A caller chooses two-sign by passing the categories from `filter_categories(node, two_sign=True)`", and
"Six operators are not fully generated. Partial: dict (no key/value swap) and raise (no inject).
Unsupported: field, class, clamp and state-extra." AC2: "Typing is by the function's observed codomain,
not by the return site. `tag(n)` returns None on one branch and a str on the other. Its tests reach only
the str branch, so the observed codomain is `{str}`. Its unexecuted `return None` gets four rewrites that
raise on None." And: "`negative_policy.unbuilt` is hashed into the two-sign policy id, but it is not a
field of `MutationPolicy`." (Detective #120-#123 are the generation side of the same gaps; their family
intent tests follow in their own commits.)

Why the obvious fix is wrong. The catalog called each premise "missing" because the observed codomain was
a bare set of type NAMES: a dict return could not show its values were hashable, a record its fields, a
class its base, a number its range, a function the exceptions it raises or the arguments it leaves alone.
Inventing a premise (guess a range, pick an attribute, pick an exception) makes the fence the engine's
guess, and a runtime dispatch that is a no-op where the premise fails is a built-in survivor. The baseline
run observes the VALUES, so the premise is obtained, not assumed: `Wesker.codomain.observe_codomain` runs
the tests once against the original and records every return (line, value), every exception that left the
function and each argument's structural state before and after every call. A premised operator is then
generated at a return site only where the perturbation was APPLIED to every value the site returned, did
not raise, and changed at least one; the attempt IS the premise (Def. 11.10). The result stays a
`frozenset[str]` (type names, `@line:type`, `@line!ok=<mode>`, `@line!no=<mode>=<why>`, `@!ok=`, `@!no=`,
`@!truncated`), the type Detective's verdict cache already carries.

What each state of the pure decisions means.
- `site_mode_standing`: function_wide (a legacy type-name observation types the whole function, as
  before) / unobserved (the baseline never executed this statement: only the always-applicable modes
  reach it) / type_outside (an observed type at this site is outside the mode's) / applies.
- `raise_standing`: local_class / base_exception / unresolvable / not_constructible / injectable.
- `clamp_standing`: non_finite / not_ordered / mixed_kinds / single_value / clamps.
- `extra_write_standing`: calls_truncated / mutated / type_varies / unmutated.
- `kill_class_of_dimension`: reach_class (an injected raise: any test that calls the function kills it,
  so the kill measures reach, not specification) / output_perturbation / not_output.
- `form_b_output_modes` gains the parameterized modes the observation admits (`raise_inject@<exception>`,
  `state_extra@<parameter>`) after the static ones, which keep their order.
An incomplete observation (a test was cut) licenses nothing but the always-applicable modes, and says so.

What this commit does. `run_function_profiling(..., two_sign=True)` adds OUTPUT and observes when the
caller supplied no observation. `_OutputMutator` filters return SITES by the codomain. Nine premised
value operators (dict swap, ctype to and from a set, field drop/bump/swap, class subclass/base, clamp) are
runtime helpers in `Wesker.mu_minus` (`perturb_return`: a value the baseline never saw comes back
unchanged, never a crash kill); raise-inject and state-extra are Form B decorators, built as source like
every Form B mutant. `MutationPolicy.unbuilt` is a field (None for the one-sign policy, `{}` for the
two-sign policy); the profile result carries `observed_codomain` and `mu_minus_withheld` (each premise
that was measured and failed, and each operator whose premise the function never offers) and `to_dict`
emits both beside `mu_minus_unbuilt`. The catalog is 36 built, 2 realized_by (0; state-noop by the
positive STATE:remove_assign and STMT, decision D2, Rem. 11.9(b), now also on
`MutationPolicy.categories["OUTPUT"]["realized_by"]`), none partial or unsupported, each premised entry
stating its premise. `docs/MU_MINUS_CATALOG.md` follows.

What was NOT closed.
- Detective still harvests only type names (`Detective/engine.py:1430`, `capture_return_types`). Until
  that call is replaced by `Wesker.codomain.observe_codomain` (same return type, same cache), a Detective
  two-sign run generates none of the premised operators and says so in `mu_minus_withheld`. Detective is
  not edited from this lane.
- Detective #140 item 3 (the page naming `unbuilt` / `mu_minus_withheld`) is Detective's.
- The intent test files of the four families follow in their own commits (the code is shared: one
  vocabulary, one catalog, one policy id).
- Pre-existing, not from this change: Detective `tests/test_absorbed_rename_intent.py::
  test_no_registry_entry_names_something_the_pair_now_declares` fails against the staging base
  (`['bound']` is now declared by Wesker's engine.py).

Policy. One-sign id unchanged (9.a5aeead0de4f). Two-sign id 9+neg.6035bc2f613d -> 9+neg.7a3fc088c5ec
(tables, premises, site-typing rule and parameterized Form B modes enter the manifest).

Pin: Wesker/engine.py::site_mode_standing FINAL ✓ COMPLETE (operator universe · modulo 2 crash-only value
gaps) · 13/13 killed · minimal cover 1 test(s) · policy 9.a5aeead0de4f

Tests: observed-codomain 33, isolated 4, catalog 7, catalog doc 5, families/form-b/policy/guards and
neighbours; 291 passed under Python 3.14 and 291 under 3.12, PYTHONUNBUFFERED unset. ruff clean under
pinned 0.16.7.
